<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Koca Sinan Pasha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1506–<a href="/years/1596">1596</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Koca Sinan Pasha was an Ottoman general and statesman of Albanian origin. He reconquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> from the Zaydi rebels in 1569 to 1571 and in 1574 took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-goulette">La Goulette</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafsid-dynasty">Hafsid dynasty</a>. He later served five times as grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> and led campaigns in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-turkish-war">Long Turkish War</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and Wallachia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/koca-sinan-pasha.jpg" alt="Koca Sinan Pasha" width="320" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arolsen_Klebeband_02_327.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Koca Sinan Pasha, &quot;Sinan the Great&quot;, was born in Topojan in Luma territory and was of Albanian origin. A Ragusan document of 1571 describes him as coming from a Roman Catholic family that had converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. His father was named Ali Bey, and he had family ties with Catholic relatives such as the Giubizzas. The Austrian orientalist Joseph von Hammer-Purgstall called him the &quot;unbridled Albanian&quot;, and Mustafa Ali of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a> repeatedly criticised him for promoting an Albanian clique in the administration.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Sinan was appointed governor of Ottoman Egypt in 1569 and until 1571 took part in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, which earned him the name Fātiḥ-i Yemen, &quot;Conqueror of Yemen&quot;. In 1580 he commanded the army against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> and was appointed grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-iii">Murad III</a>, but he was disgraced and exiled the next year after the defeat of his lieutenant Mehmed Pasha at Gori. After serving as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> he became grand vizier a second time in 1589, after the great revolt of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a>. He oversaw the execution of Petru Cercel in March 1590, having sided with Mihnea Turcitul in the contest for the throne of Wallachia. He was dismissed in 1591 after another revolt of the Janissaries.</p><p>Recalled in 1593 for a third term, he commanded the Ottoman army in the Long War against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. He took Veszprém and Palota after a siege of three or four days, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisak">Sisak</a> and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novi-sad">Novi Sad</a>. In 1594 he captured Győr. During the uprising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> in 1594 he ordered the relics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-sava">Saint Sava</a> brought from Mileševa to Belgrade, where he had them burned in order to discourage the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>. He was deposed in February 1595 and banished to Malkara. Recalled in August to lead the expedition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-the-brave">Michael the Brave</a> of Wallachia, his defeats at Călugăreni and Giurgiu and the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esztergom">Esztergom</a> cost him the seal of office on 19 November.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The death of his successor Lala Mehmed Pasha three days later made Sinan grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> for the fifth time. He died suddenly in the spring of 1596, on 3 April, and left a large fortune. He is buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> near the Grand Bazaar. He married Esmehan Hanımsultan, a granddaughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a>, and had a son and three daughters. Contemporary critics, among them Mustafa Âlî, wrote that he preferred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> for high office. At his request, in 1586, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-iii">Murad III</a> exempted five villages in Luma from all taxes. He was a major builder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a>, bridges, baths and mosques, including the fortress and mosque of Kaçanik and the Pearl Kiosk of 1590.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Koca Sinan Pasha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1574 an Ottoman fleet and army under <b>Sinan Pasha</b> and Uluj Ali took Tunis and La Goulette, the last Hafsid ruler was taken to Istanbul, and Ifriqiya became Ottoman Tunisia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1574 an Ottoman expedition under <b>Sinan Pasha</b> took Tunis and La Goulette from Spain and its Hafsid client, ending the Hafsid Sultanate.</blockquote><a href="/state/ottoman-tunisia">Ottoman Tunisia</a></li><li><blockquote>A great revolt under al-Mutahhar in 1567 almost expelled them, until <b>Sinan Pasha</b> reconquered the country in 1569-1570.</blockquote><a href="/state/yemen-eyalet">Yemen Eyalet</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Dibër District, in what is now <a href="/countries/albania">Albania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.72,20.35&amp;year=1564" aria-label="Dibër District on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5046 677 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Koca Sinan Pasha"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5496 947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5496 947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.72,20.35&amp;year=1564">What was here in 1564? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
